<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sulh-i-kul</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sulh-i-kul, meaning peace with all or universal tolerance, was a principle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a> associated with the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>. It aimed to govern a diverse population of Muslims, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and others without favouring one faith. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> treat it as central to Akbar's governance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sulh-i-kul.jpg" alt="Sulh-i-kul" width="320" height="486" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Akbar.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
